     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "Uber processes millions of rides without owning a single mapping satellite. You think giant apps build every single feature from scratch inside their own servers, but they actually rely on digital messengers called APIs to fetch outside data in milliseconds, because application programming interfaces act as standardized translators between completely separate software systems, so a client app simply sends a request payload to a host server, which immediately returns structured data like clean JSON back down the line. Third party tools quietly handle critical app functions like credit card processing and live geolocation, so companies monetize these very connections by charging developers for metered access through secret authentication keys that lock down every single request. The entire modern internet is just invisible software talking to other software."
    }
